  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 1100 AM CDT Fri Jul 31 2026\n\nThe gusty northerly winds will decrease in magnitude after 7 pm tonight.\nA light northerly wind is expected through the overnight hours and\nthen again tomorrow. The northerly winds will advect drier air \nacross the FA over the weekend with lower dewpoints. This will \nbe good news, as humidity levels will be lower Saturday and \nSunday. Highs will be close to near normal values with highs \npeaking in the lower to mid 90s. Lows both mornings will be \nmainly in the 60s Saturday evening and then a mix of 60s to 70s \nSunday morning. No storms are expected both days.\n\nA 500 hPa anticyclone will move from the SW US to over the greater\nregion through next week. This will support the notion of a mainly\ndry forecast and hot highs. Highs will trend warmer each day. Upper\n90s to low 100s are likely for much of the next business week. Lows\nwill be warm with more 70s rather than 60s as each morning progresses.\nThe NBM has very low slight pops out west Tuesday evening. A look\nat the global models and their ensemble counterparts do not support\nthese storms chances much. Much of the ensembles are dry for next\nweek. With such a strong anticyclone over the FA, it would make more\nsense not to see any storms than to see them.\n\nLooking beyond next week, the ensembles continue to paint the picture\nof a prevailing mid level ridge across the Great Plains. This would\nsuggest a dry and hot forecast with temperatures trending to be\nabove normal as we head into the middle portion of August.",
